# stack/autoinstall/wall/packages.list — every package the WALL PANEL installs.
#
# The hub's ../packages.list carries the full argument for why this list lives
# in a file instead of in the user-data's `packages:`. The short version: that
# key runs BEFORE any late-command, so nothing a late-command adds can help it,
# and a single entry left there is enough to make the install reach for the
# archive and die there when the network is not up.
#
# Five things read this file and none of them retypes it:
#
#   vmtest/export-apt.sh                     bakes the closure into an apt repo
#   wall/user-data late-command 3c           installs FROM that repo, reading
#                                            this file off the payload
#   vmtest/assert-installed.sh               asserts each name on the booted panel
#   vmtest/lib/common.sh (assert_electron_runtime_deps)
#                                            refuses to bake a shell artifact
#                                            whose DT_NEEDED list is not covered
#   vmtest/test-wall-artifact.sh             installs exactly this list in a
#                                            container and runs ldd against it
#
# FORMAT: one package per line, `#` comments to end of line, blank lines ignored.
#
# REBUILD THE BAKED REPO WHEN YOU CHANGE THIS FILE:
#   bash vmtest/export-apt.sh --target wall --out .out-wall/apt

ca-certificates
curl
cage                   # single-surface Wayland kiosk compositor (the session)
seatd                  # seat management fallback if the logind seat path fails
xdg-utils
fonts-dejavu-core
util-linux             # rtcwake — the PRIMARY wake source (D-W4)
network-manager        # quirk 5: powersave off, and the netplan renderer
# THE PANEL HAD NO RESOLVER AT ALL UNTIL 2026-08-08, and nothing said so.
# `systemctl is-active systemd-resolved` answered `not-found`: the package was
# never installed, so /etc/resolv.conf sat as a DANGLING symlink to
# ../run/systemd/resolve/stub-resolv.conf and glibc had nowhere to ask. networkd
# knew the nameserver perfectly well — `networkctl status` printed
# `DNS: <hub>` — it just had nothing to write it into.
#
# HOW IT PRESENTED, and this is why it belongs in a package list with a
# paragraph attached: the wall went BLANK. Electron failed the kiosk URL with
# ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED, wall-sync could not resolve the hub for the music mount
# or Mini-serv for the frame pull, both manifests were therefore missing, and the
# only visible symptom on a wall-mounted display was a dark screen. Every one of
# those looks like a different fault and all of them were this.
#
# The hub never showed it because its heavier package set pulls resolved in
# transitively; the panel's deliberately lean one does not. Neither list named
# it, so neither install was actually asking for it — the hub was lucky.
systemd-resolved       # /etc/resolv.conf; without it the panel resolves NOTHING
# AND ITS SIBLINGS, for the reason the hub's list now spells out at length:
# every binary from the systemd source Depends on systemd (= ${binary:Version})
# EXACTLY, so naming one drags the whole family to the archive's version and apt
# DELETES any sibling the baked repo cannot match.
#
# MEASURED ON THIS PANEL, 2026-08-08, after it installed and came up looking
# perfectly healthy: `udev` survived at 8.16 (the wall's heavier set happened to
# carry it), but `libnss-systemd` and `systemd-timesyncd` were both left
# `deinstall ok config-files` at 8.12 — REMOVED. The hub, whose list did not
# drag udev, lost udev too and reached emergency.target on every boot.
#
# So the panel has been running with NO TIME SYNCHRONISATION, on a device whose
# entire job is to display a wall clock and honour a sleep window. Nothing
# reported it: the box boots, the kiosk paints, and the clock is simply wrong by
# however far the RTC has drifted. That is the failure mode this repo keeps
# meeting — a machine that looks healthy while missing something load-bearing.
udev                   # /dev/disk/by-uuid/* — local-fs.target cannot resolve without it
systemd-timesyncd      # the wall shows a CLOCK; it was being removed at install
libnss-systemd         # base-installed sibling; strict-versioned against systemd

# ── the media sync (OI-15): the panel PULLS, so it needs a cifs client ───────
cifs-utils             # mount.cifs — the read-only mount of the media share
rsync                  # the mirror itself (`rsync -a --delete`)
python3                # wall-media-manifest.py (JSON escaping, not bash).
                       # Present anyway (cloud-init needs it); listed so the
                       # dependency is explicit rather than inherited by luck.
alsa-utils             # audio sink pinning (WSN-012 lives in the app's runbook)
pulseaudio-utils
va-driver-all          # VA-API decode; quirk 6 says PROVE it is engaged
vainfo

# grim — screenshot the RUNNING kiosk over SSH, from inside the compositor:
#   sudo -u panel env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000 WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0 grim /tmp/panel.png
# The panel is on a wall, often asleep, and hard to photograph; "send me a
# picture of what it is showing" is otherwise a trip across the office. It is
# also the ONLY way to see a cage session on real hardware — /dev/fb0 is the
# text console underneath and shows nothing once cage takes over KMS, and
# Hyper-V's thumbnail API does not exist off a VM. ~50 KB, wlr-screencopy.
grim

unattended-upgrades    # same hands-off patching posture as the hub
# Explicit here for the same reason it is explicit on the hub: `ssh:
# install-server` is an apt install at the pre-late-command moment and is now
# `false`. This line is the panel's only door once it is on a wall.
openssh-server

# ── the Electron runtime's shared libraries (IF-005 / PKG-1) ────────────────
# The shell artifact ships its own Electron binary but NOT the ~24 system
# libraries it links against — those are the image's job. Every name below is
# derived from the shipped binary's own DT_NEEDED list and is enforced:
# ./electron-runtime-deps.tsv maps soname -> package, and the wall builder
# (vmtest/lib/common.sh assert_electron_runtime_deps) refuses to bake an
# artifact whose dependencies are not all covered here. Do not prune this list
# by eye — a missing library is a BLACK WALL, not an error message: Electron
# exits before painting, wall-kiosk.sh restarts it every 3 s, and `[ -x ]` stays
# true so the NOT INSTALLED screen never fires either.
# The `t64` suffixes are 24.04's 64-bit-time_t renames; the old names do not
# exist in noble and apt would fail the install outright.
libx11-6
libxcomposite1
libxdamage1
libxext6
libxfixes3
libxrandr2
libasound2t64
libatk1.0-0t64
libatk-bridge2.0-0t64
libatspi2.0-0t64
libcairo2
libcups2t64
libdbus-1-3
libexpat1
libgbm1
libglib2.0-0t64
libgtk-3-0t64
libnspr4
libnss3
libpango-1.0-0
libudev1
libxcb1
libxkbcommon0
